  topic: 'New Business: Student Rep Exception for Superintendent Search'
  desc: The board considered and approved a one-time exception to policy BBAB-R, allowing
    student representatives to meet with superintendent semi-finalists under strict
    conditions, including confidentiality training and no voting power.
  children:
  - seconds: 6734
    speaker: Rosemarie DeAngelis
    text: Student Representatives Kabisa and Davison expressed sincere interest in
      the superintendent selection process. This one-time exception, developed with
      legal counsel, allows them to participate in semi-finalist interviews. They
      must attend confidentiality and bias training, agree in writing not to share
      information, and will not be involved in deliberations or voting. Candidates
      must also consent to their presence.
  - seconds: 6818
    speaker: Angela Kabisa
    text: Alex and I are very grateful for this consideration. We wanted to ask if
      it's okay for us to gather input from other students about what they'd like
      to see in their next superintendent, even with the confidentiality requirements.
  - seconds: 6842
    speaker: Rosemarie DeAngelis
    text: Absolutely, you can gather input from students prior to the interviews.
      You also have scheduled virtual meetings with the Zeal team to provide your
      perspective. The key is that once the interview process begins, you cannot share
      any information about the candidates or the interviews with anyone outside of
      executive session.
  - seconds: 6883
    speaker: Claire Holman
    text: I think this is a great idea and I'm very supportive of it. It's unfortunate
      that our student representatives' votes don't count, as I believe their input
      is valuable. I wish that could be changed someday, even if it's a matter of
      state law or city charter.
- time: 01:58:31
  seconds: 7111
  topic: 'New Business: Endorsement of City Council Immigration Resolve'
  desc: 'The board unanimously voted to endorse the South Portland City Council''s
    Resolve #5-25/26, which addresses federal immigration enforcement activity and
    community safety, acknowledging its significant impact on immigrant students and
    families.'
  children:
  - seconds: 7180
    speaker: Eleni Richardson
    text: I'm glad the board is taking formal action to recognize the intense, traumatic
      experience our community has endured. I'm grateful for South Portland's community
      protecting and supporting neighbors. It's important to make public statements,
      but also to take concrete action. No child should be afraid to come to school,
      and our staff's response, walking children home and riding buses with them,
      was exceptional.
  - seconds: 7302
    speaker: Rosemarie DeAngelis
    text: I was present at the City Council meeting when this resolve passed, including
      the amendment to request a 60-day moratorium on evictions from the governor.
      I met with Mayor Tipton to discuss the school board's endorsement, feeling it
      was crucial to publicly acknowledge the significant impact on our immigrant,
      refugee, and asylum students and families. This board's support signifies that
      we see, recognize, and acknowledge the situation, offering our full backing.
- time: 02:03:52
  seconds: 7432
  topic: 'New Business: Request for Elementary School Reconfiguration Proposal'
  desc: The board approved a motion to request a formal proposal from the superintendent
    regarding the benefits and impact of elementary school reconfiguration, to be
    discussed at the March 2nd budget workshop.
  children:
  - seconds: 7476
    speaker: Daniel Feller
    text: This was the first cost-saving idea presented at the budget forum, and I've
      heard significant community feedback, mostly against it. Reconfiguration is
      a major decision, changing decades of neighborhood schools, and requires extensive
      community input. I support a formal study to understand cost savings but emphasize
      that this cannot be solely a central office and board decision; it needs a broad
      community-led approach.
  - seconds: 7590
    speaker: Eleni Richardson
    text: I agree with Member Feller; this proposal needs ample time, space, and data
      for thorough discussion. We need a clear breakdown of savings and startup costs.
      I encourage the community to attend the workshop and continue emailing the board
      with concerns. I also requested that the workshop address why closing an elementary
      school was deemed not a viable option, to understand the underlying assumptions.
  - seconds: 7734
    speaker: Rosemarie DeAngelis
    text: I believe this is a fabulous idea and fully support it, as I think it's
      good for kids. The concept of 'neighborhood schools' has changed significantly
      since 1977, and kids are often more flexible than parents about change. This
      board must make tough decisions about budget cuts, and while public input is
      vital, the ultimate decision rests with us, not a public referendum.
